Vfu1ZLZJ1RSwVl8TO7Pujp_fBDgmVZADCqjzJIwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke27152ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;VkVdxPOPqA3iyTOMY87nnK27Rhf1ECQXb8ujeAL8k7kƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;hYwji50tcxNbP2nOzfAI_BEY8tVdh69dkXLC69bhY-Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkd3023ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;XtzfjkMSxe9JBoN6TLXE-fg4XVCjMLE0m1X12ptdPeIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgmessageƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_bzYJ4m-m2NPbxGjSZPHZNmURpKbzto3TA-9HXGXI10ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke11592ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;T9d66cRZT89ySeu28mTSqKKGV28tM_qE937hGekcDQoƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;z1WqvM9bOBC1Zdpl4lib-CqS4XlSXtVXqNBlrON4QH0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;FGtc9vmdCoGd1dgl-uFJ9MmZ08waubZ026KB8zwFOlcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TxGrN7gs4PkY1kwy2XuUSJ2tgYsOTz6xjylmjmFnICMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DEz6u0EMU6vmXr8qpQVzHcR6ipykcxZDRAT7wZStFDkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QYogSAi-GDGUPkkWjxHC8GuDDsTWC-BNpc6J0OMsu5Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$3e596634-d7f7-4942-ab1b-c2e7f78c919eƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;3PXb6NyPYqTGG7jLOcATvHnMC9HErHrF24rdtaz9VAkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky2Œ{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"writer-model â€” regenerate article body and frontmatter summary from the licensed evidence cards","summary":"The manuscript contains pervasive internal pipeline claim-ID markers that would render as raw text for readers, fails to name the current exhibition event in the lede, omits the gallery, location, and run dates from the body entirely, and makes factual claims before the required source citation appears. These defects are structural and compounded by 14 near-duplicate-proximity warnings; bounded text replacement cannot repair them without generating new prose, so a writer-model replay is required.","requiredRepairs":["Strip all [claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:N] internal pipeline markers from the body and from the frontmatter summary field before publication; these render as literal text to readers and are not valid publication copy.","Rewrite the lede so that the very first sentence names the exhibition event concretely â€” the exhibition title 'The Mermaid's Revenge - Paintings 1991-2001', the venue Tibor de Nagy gallery (11 Rivington Street, Lower East Side, Manhattan), and the run through July 31 â€” not a career-profile generalization.","Move the inline source citation to before the first factual claim; the current draft makes two factual sentences before the citation appears, violating the citation-is-precondition rule.","Replace 'frequently described as cheerfully sinister' with attribution to the single Hyperallergic reviewer's characterization; the source establishes one reviewer's description, not a critical consensus, and the manuscript strengthens the source modality without warrant.","Weave exhibition-specific material â€” at minimum the exhibition title, gallery, location, and closing date â€” into the body; the current draft reads as a timeless career overview with no temporal news hook.","Resolve near-duplicate-proximity across the three body sections by eliminating redundant sentence-level restatements; 14 instances are flagged by the copy-editing gate, including multiple sentences in each section that re-introduce the same palette/style/duality framing already established earlier in the same paragraph."],"repairTools":[],"report":"\n## Editorial Assessment â€” reporting-source-1-for-trevor-winkfield-cheerfully-sinister-worlds\n\n### Source and Assignment Review\n\nThe single admitted source is a Hyperallergic critical review (published 2026-07-17) of the exhibition \"Trevor Winkfield: The Mermaid's Revenge - Paintings 1991-2001\" at Tibor de Nagy gallery, 11 Rivington Street, Lower East Side, Manhattan, running through July 31. The source is genuine, dated, and contains concrete and attributable material: specific painting titles with years, gallery and address, physical dimensions, the reviewer's named characterizations of style and tone, and biographical facts traceable to specific evidence cards. The source is adequate to support a 300â€“650-word current-news-brief anchored in the exhibition event. The source layer is sound.\n\nThe assignment targets a culture news brief about this exhibition and its critical reception. The evidence budget â€” 20 claims, 300â€“650 words â€” is appropriate to the material. The assignment layer is sound.\n\n### Deterministic Standards\n\nThe deterministic reading marks the manuscript publishable (no blocking gate failures) but flags 14 near-duplicate-proximity warnings across all three body sections and one demand-lens-missing-h2 warning. These warnings are substantive: the 14 near-duplicate flags correspond to genuine prose-level repetition visible in the manuscript text, not instrument noise. The gate layer is clear as a matter of deterministic processing, but the warnings describe real defects that editorial review must address.\n\n### Critical Defects Found in Manuscript\n\n**1. Pipeline claim-ID markers left in publication copy (publication-blocking)**\n\nEvery factual sentence in the body ends with unstripped internal markers, for example:\n> \"â€¦has been marked by a consistent engagement with the intersections of poetry and art [claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:2, claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:10].\"\n\nThese identifiers appear in every paragraph of the body and also in the frontmatter `summary` field. In standard Markdown, bracket expressions not in hyperlink syntax render as literal text. A reader would see `[claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:14, claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:15]` inline. This is a publication-blocking defect; the manuscript has not been stripped of its editorial-traceability annotation layer before submission. Because the markers appear in 12 or more discrete body locations and in the frontmatter summary, this cannot be repaired by a small number of bounded text replacements â€” it requires a clean regeneration pass.\n\n**2. Lede does not name the event (structural rule violation)**\n\nThe instructions require the very first sentence to name the event concretely: who, where, what. The first sentence is: \"The artist Trevor Winkfield has long inhabited a dual identity as both a visual practitioner and a literary figure, producing works that operate with a unique, meticulous precision.\" This is a career-profile opener, not an event opener. Neither the exhibition title, the gallery, the address, nor the closing date appears anywhere in the manuscript body. The article is structured as a timeless career overview that could have been written at any point in Winkfield's career, with no news hook anchoring it to the current exhibition. A reader following a link from this article would have no idea that a specific show is open in Manhattan through July 31.\n\n**3. Source citation placed after first factual claim (citation-is-precondition violation)**\n\nThe instructions state: \"cite THE STORY as [Exact Story Title](its-exact-url) before making its first factual claim.\" The second sentence â€” \"His career, which began in earnest while attending school in Leeds and later moving to New York in 1969\" â€” is a concrete factual claim. The source citation first appears in the third sentence: \"As documented in [Trevor Winkfield's Cheerfully Sinister Worlds]â€¦\". Two sentences of factual content precede the citation, violating the precondition rule. The cited title matches the source exactly, which is correct; only the placement is wrong.\n\n**4. Attribution strengthening of single reviewer's characterization**\n\nThe manuscript writes: \"The resulting imagery is frequently described as cheerfully sinister, a duality that speaks to the artist's ability to imbue artificial settings with a sense of mysterious, if incomprehensible, activity.\"\n\nThe phrase \"frequently described as\" implies a critical consensus. The source establishes one reviewer's characterization: \"He has conjured an artificial world â€” simultaneously brightly colored, slightly sinister, and cheerfully incomprehensible.\" This is one named publication's description of the work in one review. The manuscript strengthens the modality from a single source attribution to a recurring critical consensus without warrant. The claim should be attributed visibly to the Hyperallergic reviewer's language.\n\n**5. Unattributed evaluative adjective**\n\nThe manuscript characterizes Winkfield as \"an insightful writer, contributing to a body of criticism.\" The source says he has \"written smart and original pieces.\" \"Insightful\" is the manuscript's own paraphrase of an evaluative quality, not the source's term, and appears without attribution.\n\n**6. Near-duplicate proximity â€” 14 flagged instances**\n\nThe four sentences of the \"Systems of Representation\" section are particularly redundant. Each sentence introduces a variation on the same idea â€” Winkfield's resistance to interpretation, the viewer's engagement with artificial structures, the self-referential logic of the imagery â€” without advancing the argument. The \"Meticulous Stylistic Logic\" section similarly defines the palette in one sentence and then re-characterizes the same palette feature in the next two sentences. The \"Connections to Literary Traditions\" section describes his role as a writer in three consecutive sentences that partially overlap. These are not minor stylistic preferences; they inflate word count without adding licensed factual content, and they directly reflect the warning pattern flagged by the copy-editing gate.\n\n**7. Frontmatter summary contains raw claim IDs**\n\nThe YAML `summary` field reads: \"â€¦Winkfield's paintings are defined by a specific, saturated palette and a flat, volumetric style that eschews traditional depth in favor of a packed, illustrative surface [claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:14, claim:trevor-winkfield-s-cheerfully-sinister-worlds:15].\" This field is publication-facing (used in feed distribution and syndication per the pipeline configuration). Internal claim-ID markers in the summary field would surface in feeds and social cards.\n\n### Evidence Audit â€” Material Claims Against Witnessed Source\n\nClaims properly grounded in the witnessed evidence:\n- Winkfield earned a master's degree from the Royal College of Art in 1967, moved to New York in 1969, published the poetry magazine Juillard (1968â€“72): supported by claim:2 and the source summary.\n- Collaborated with poets John Ashbery, Charles North, Ron Padgett, Harry Matthews in 1976: supported by claim:6.\n- Published translation of Raymond Roussel's *How I Wrote Certain of My Books* in 1977: supported by claim:7.\n- Roussel wrote using homophones: supported by claim:8.\n- Works on linen in acrylic; the exhibition contains eight pieces (1991â€“2001): supported by claim:12 and the source summary.\n- Specific paintings \"The Student\" (1999), \"The Navigator\" (2000), \"The Astrologer\" (2001), \"Self-Portrait\" (2001): supported by claim:17.\n- \"The Student\" figure detail (helmet, fish, lock, long-handled pot, lightbulb, snowflake tokens): supported by claim:19.\n- Tokens reappear above a pink and pale-blue mountain in an open window: supported by claim:20.\n- Writings collected in *Georges Braque and Others: The Selected Art Writings of Trevor Winkfield, 1990â€“2009* (2014): supported by claim:18.\n- Worked for Arthur Cohen on Dada/Surrealism catalogs: supported by claim:3.\n\nClaims that are over-stated or not adequately supported:\n- \"frequently described as cheerfully sinister\" â€” overstates the single Hyperallergic reviewer's characterization as a critical consensus (as noted above).\n- The manuscript says critics \"searched for specific meanings within his paintings\"; the source says critics \"tried to connect Winkfield's collage-like paintings of artificial worlds with Roussel's wildly improbable novels.\" The manuscript paraphrase is broader than what claim:9 licenses.\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe failure layer is **writer-model**. The source and assignment are adequate to support the commission. The instructions are explicit on all violated rules (citation precondition, first-sentence event naming, claim-ID traceability as internal-only). The gate layer cleared on a deterministic reading but correctly flagged the near-duplicate proximity issues as warnings. The writer model (gemini-3.1-flash-lite) produced copy that: left internal pipeline traceability markers in publication-facing fields; structured the article as a career overview rather than an event news brief; placed the source citation after the first factual sentence; and padded the final section with near-duplicate restatements. These failures are intrinsic to the draft's architecture and cannot be resolved by bounded text-replacement repair â€” they require a clean writer-model pass with the same evidence cards and corrective instruction.\n\nThe headline (\"Trevor Winkfield's Precision Paradox\"), generated by a separate headline model, does not preserve the source event's organizing terms and introduces a frame not supported by the source. The headline should also be regenerated in the replay pass.\n\n### Corrective Instructions for Replay\n\nThe replay should: (1) open the first sentence with the exhibition name, gallery, location, and dates; (2) place the Hyperallergic source link before the first factual claim; (3) attribute stylistic characterizations visibly to the Hyperallergic reviewer rather than presenting them as consensus; (4) strip claim-ID markers from all publication-facing fields before output; (5) have each body section add new concrete material (specific paintings, dimensions, specific writing titles) rather than restating the style/duality frame from the previous section; (6) not use \"frequently described as\" or equivalent consensus-implying language for single-source characterizations.\n"}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;uNlWHom-yIlMsPwDYq1xYI0ZqmwRP-XBq929Y9gCAWQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;MjYz9XJL8M8Mc-IlbnRAA9AapUT26oIIE8zOy5TUREAƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8KhrTqGKkoh0i1EEWLI_H2nhnSOsyL0TWsLmvNXZvDIƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;wWT6L5BltADBq-jriq8olplkbvjg5aAVoIy_yM29bbIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///